"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a favorable pathological outcome, demonstrating negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was 4.3. The surgical findings, including Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pT2 stage with no extraprostatic extension, are favorable prognostic factors. Conversely, the missing biopsy grade and ISUP report introduce uncertainty regarding the true tumor aggressiveness. The nodal status is documented as negative, which is a positive finding. The primary uncertainty in the prognosis lies with the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as this information is currently absent. A postoperative PSA measurement and its trend would significantly revise the prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence risk. Missing postoperative PSA data prevents a definitive assessment of the immediate post-operative risk profile. The fixed estimated horizon is 77.09 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 61.68–92.51 months; its coverage has not been validated."
